                0.357
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 12.2: Grouping the organisms"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.361,
                0.77,
                0.466
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_table_6.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>The grouping</td><td>Which organisms fit</td><td>What feature or pattern</td></tr><tr><td>criterion</td><td>in this group?</td><td>helped you decide?</td></tr><tr><td>Carnivore</td><td>Eagle, tiger, leopard</td><td>Eating habits</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.473,
            0.772,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may notice that the same organism can fit into different groups depending on the criterion you choose. This leads to an important question: how do scientists decide which features to use while grouping the living organisms? This question leads us to the idea of classification, a systematic way of organising the Earth’s living diversity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.772,
            0.501,
            0.829,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.772,
                0.501,
                0.829,
                0.536
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.567,
            0.603,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.3.1 Some criteria to classify living organisms"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.833,
            0.499,
            0.944,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.588,
            0.764,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Scientists often look at broad and easily visible features, and then look at more detailed features. They use a number of characteristics to group living organisms, some of which are given below:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.642,
            0.762,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. External features — visible characteristics, such as shape, size and body organisation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.787,
            0.54,
            0.939,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. If many organisms share common features, could they also share a common ancestry?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.68,
            0.598,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Mode of nutrition — autotrophic or heterotrophic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.7,
            0.774,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Internal structures — skeletal patterns, presence or absence of organs and different types of tissues."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.738,
            0.773,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Cell structure — whether the organism is unicellular or multicellular, the cell is eukaryote or prokaryote, presence or absence of cell wall."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.776,
            0.63,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Ecological role — producer, consumer, or decomposer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.796,
            0.581,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Reproduction — asexual and/or sexual methods."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.817,
            0.774,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Genetic similarity — similarities in inherited features, which scientists study in detail using DNA."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.854,
            0.775,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These features help in understanding the similarities and differences among organisms. Similar features in organisms suggest that they have evolved from common ancestors."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
